4.comparison between shown UX and hidden UX and possible QA missing out possible bug area-----Activity Timeline UX: What is ShownThe UI is designed to be a visual, chronological audit trail, often utilizing color-coding and filtering to highlight key events.Detailed Interaction Logs: Records of who did what, when (calls, tasks, meetings), and how (e.g., API, workflow, manual user action).Field Changes: Displays old and new values for modified fields.Email Sentiment/Status: Visual cues showing if an email was sent, opened, or if a picklist field was updated.Contextual Filtering: Ability to filter by entry type (notes, tasks), user, or time period.Visual Indicators: Color-coded status (green for completed, blue for ongoing, yellow for upcoming).Notes and Attachments: Direct display of notes, often showing which note was added in response to which event.
2. Activity Timeline UX: What is HiddenData Older than 3 Years: While it tracks up to 3 years on-screen, older history is archived and not visible in the UI without direct Zoho support.Record-Level Security Limits: If a user does not have permission to view a specific related record, that interaction may be hidden, even if it directly impacted the main record.Specific API Payloads: The timeline shows that an API update occurred but does not display the full JSON payload or the exact API response body unless specifically queried via API.System-Generated "Noise": Minor or repetitive system updates might be bundled or hidden to improve readability.Records Without Dates: Records lacking start/end dates may not appear in the Timeline view, only in the list view.
3)3. What a QA Engineer Might Miss (UI-Only Testing)Relying solely on the UI for testing can lead to missing critical backend or functional issues.Workflow/Automation Failures: A workflow might fail silently in the background, but the UI might make it look like the record was updated. The timeline might not show why a field changed.API-to-UI Latency: The timeline might show an update happened via crm_api, but the data might not yet be reflected in other parts of the UI, or the sequence of actions might not accurately reflect the latency.Incorrect Field Triggers: A field might be updated by a Blueprint or workflow rule, but the timeline might not correctly attribute the "source" of that change, attributing it to the user rather than the workflow.Deleted Data Context: When a record is deleted, the timeline might not show the full history of what was there before, only that a deletion event occurred.Field-Level Permission Gaps: A user might be able to trigger a field change via a button, but cannot see the change in the timeline, which QA might interpret as a failure to update.Data Migration Errors: If data was imported, the timeline shows the import date, but not the original, historical activity timestamps.



